[goal] Fix the hook bus so `tool.after` hooks run in order, always run, and failures propagate.

[action] One bug in `src/bus.ts`: `emit` used `list.forEach(async handler => { await handler(payload); })`. `forEach` does not await, so every handler started at once (out of order), `emit` resolved before any finished (hooks "never ran" before the next emit), and rejections were unhandled promises (silently ignored). Replaced it with a snapshot + sequential `for...of` loop:

```ts
const list = [...(this.handlers.get(event) ?? [])];
for (const handler of list) {
  await handler(payload);
}
```

The snapshot also implements the README rule that mid-emit subscribe/unsubscribe only counts from the next emit on. Public API, tests, and package.json untouched; no browser was opened.

[result] `npm test`: 2 pass, 0 fail. A 7-point scratch check (deleted after) verified each README rule against the fixed bus: sequential subscription order with async handlers; `emit` resolves only after the last handler; a rejecting handler rejects `emit` with the same error and later handlers don't run; self-unsubscribe mid-emit doesn't stop others; late subscription doesn't run in the same emit; and the audit plugin end-to-end produces exactly `notice once → redacted log → redacted log`.

[next] Nothing — the fix is complete and verified. Assumption stated: "sometimes a hook never runs" was the same root cause (emit resolving early, so dependent work saw unredacted/unordered output), not a separate missing-handler bug; the plugin itself is correct once the bus is.

